{%- comment -%}
  ─── ONE HOME RAIL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  A heading, a "see all" link, and a row of photographs.

  It renders NOTHING if `rail_products` is short. That is the whole graceful-
  degradation rule in one place: a rail with two photographs in a five-column
  row is a page that looks broken, and a shop with thin stock should show fewer
  sections rather than emptier ones. The caller decides the threshold; this
  snippet enforces it so no call site can forget.

  ACCEPTS
    rail_title     heading text
    rail_url       where "See all" goes — the COMPLETE listing for this section,
                   which is not always the same place the products came from
    rail_products  the products to show
    rail_limit     how many to show
    rail_min       render nothing below this many
    rail_count     accepted and ignored — the button says "See all", nothing more
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign n = rail_products.size
  assign floor = rail_min | default: 4
-%}
{%- if n >= floor -%}
  <section class="mc-rail">
    <div class="mc-rail__head">
      <h2 class="mc-rail__title">{{ rail_title }}</h2>
      <a class="mc-rail__more" href="{{ rail_url }}">
        {%- comment -%} No count. "See all 564" made the button about the
          number rather than the invitation, and the number is the least
          interesting thing on it. {%- endcomment -%}
        See all &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="mc-rail__track">
      {%- for product in rail_products limit: rail_limit -%}
        {%- assign lazy = 'lazy' -%}
        {%- if forloop.index <= 2 -%}{%- assign lazy = 'eager' -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- render 'marathon-card', product: product, lazy: lazy,
            sizes_attr: '(min-width: 990px) 19vw, (min-width: 600px) 27vw, 44vw' -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </section>
{%- endif -%}
